    name: "Checks",
    section: "C",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "A check is a roll of 1d20 plus the appropriate modifiers, compared against a Difficulty Class; higher is better. Unlike attack rolls and saving throws, a natural 20 is not an automatic success and a natural 1 is not an automatic failure. The book's example DC scale: very easy 0, easy 5, average 10, tough 15, challenging 20, formidable 25, heroic 30, nearly impossible 40. Conditional modifiers use four levers, all built on the number 2: a +2 circumstance bonus when conditions improve performance, a -2 circumstance penalty when conditions hamper it, lowering the DC by 2 when the task itself is made easier, and raising the DC by 2 when the task is made harder — conditions affecting the performer change the modifier, while conditions affecting the required performance change the DC; extreme circumstances can exceed these values, and multiple conditions accumulate. Trying again is generally allowed and can continue indefinitely, but the consequences of failure may apply, retrying can be useless after a failure on a given task, and success makes further successes on the same task meaningless. Degrees of success and failure usually matter only for the information gained, but failing by 5 or more triggers extra consequences where they exist: Balance and Climb — fall; Craft — ruin the materials; Disable Device — trigger the device; Spot — false information when reading lips; Swim — go underwater; Use Rope — fail to secure a grappling hook; others at the DM's discretion. Skill checks combine skill ranks, the key ability modifier, and other modifiers (racial bonuses, armor check penalty, feats). Untrained checks are made without ranks; trained-only skills cannot be used untrained. Ability checks add the ability modifier and cover tasks no skill applies to; some comparisons are straight contests with no roll at all (there is no check to see who is taller or stronger). Caster level checks roll 1d20 + caster level for the spell or effect (class level plus modifiers, or a Use Magic Device result - 20); they are used to overcome spell resistance, for dispel checks, and to activate a scroll above your level. Taking 10 and taking 20 do NOT apply to caster level checks, though they do apply to skill and ability checks. Opposed checks: the higher result wins; a tie goes to the higher modifier; if the modifiers are also equal, roll again. Taking 10: when you aren't threatened or distracted, treat the die roll as 10 — a safety measure for routine tasks; threats and distractions such as combat prevent it. Taking 20: with plenty of time (2 minutes for a check that normally takes 1 round or a standard action), no threats or distractions, and no penalty for failure, treat the roll as 20; it assumes many failures along the way, takes 20 times as long as a single check, and cannot be used on any check with consequences for failure; common take-20 skills are Escape Artist, Open Lock, and Search. Combining effort on simultaneous checks toward the same purpose: some events are individual (one character's Climb result doesn't affect another's); a character with 5 or more ranks in the skill being used can take a -4 penalty on their own check to grant allies a +2 circumstance bonus on the same check for the same task, or with 15 or more ranks take a -10 penalty to grant +5 instead; allies must be within 30 feet and able to see and hear the helper; eligible skills include Balance, Bluff, Climb, Craft, Diplomacy, Escape Artist, Handle Animal, Hide, Move Silently, Ride, Search, Survival, and Swim, and the DM can allow others. (The Aid Another rule itself is the separate Aid Another topic.) Skill synergy: 5 or more ranks in a skill grant a +2 bonus on checks with each synergistic skill as noted in the skill descriptions, sometimes only for specific uses of the skill.",
