      "Concealment applies when nothing physically blocks a blow or shot but something interferes with accuracy — fog, smoke, shadowy illumination, darkness, tall grass, foliage, or magical effects. Determining it for a ranged attack: choose a corner of your square; if any line from that corner to any corner of the target's square passes through a square or border that provides concealment, the target has concealment. For a melee attack against an adjacent target, the target has concealment if its space is entirely within a concealment-granting effect; melee attacks against a non-adjacent target (such as with a reach weapon) use the ranged-attack determination. Some magical effects grant concealment against all attacks regardless of intervening concealment. Effect: an otherwise successful attack against a concealed subject has a 20% miss chance; the defending side rolls percentile dice to see whether the attack is avoided (the rolls can be made simultaneously to speed play, and the DM keeps monster and NPC miss-chance rolls secret when appropriate). Multiple concealment conditions don't stack — if a creature would have miss chances from multiple sources (such as being incorporeal and concealed), only the highest applies. You can use concealment to make a Hide check; without concealment you usually need cover to hide. Total concealment: you have line of effect to the target but no line of sight; you cannot attack such an opponent directly, but you can attack into the square you think it occupies; you cannot make attacks of opportunity against a totally concealed opponent even if you know which square it occupies; and a successful attack into the occupied square has a 50% miss chance. Ignoring concealment: some attributes negate it — for example, darkness provides no concealment against a creature with darkvision. Degrees: the DM may adjust the miss chance up or down for unusual situations.",
