    section: "C",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "A Concentration check (Constitution-based) is required when you are distracted during an activity that needs your full attention: casting a spell, concentrating on an active spell, directing a spell, using a spell-like ability, or using a skill that provokes attacks of opportunity. A distraction matters if it strikes between starting and completing an act of 1 round or longer, or in response to initiating the activity (such as an attack of opportunity or a readied or contingent attack); with no distraction, usually no check is needed. DCs: injury — 10 + damage dealt; continuous damage — 10 + half the continuous damage last dealt; distracted by a nondamaging spell — the spell's saving throw DC; entangled — 15; grappling or pinned — 20; vigorous motion — 10; violent motion — 15; extraordinarily violent motion — 20; bad weather — 5; severe weather — 10. When the distraction interrupts casting, concentrating on, or directing a spell, add the spell's level to the DC. Details: half of continuous damage is treated as occurring during your actions, but if the last damage dealt was the final damage the effect could deal, it cannot distract; repeated damage is not continuous damage. A nondamaging distracting spell that normally allows no saving throw uses the save DC it would have if it did allow one. The motion ladder: vigorous motion is a moving mount, a bouncy wagon ride, a small boat in rough water, or being belowdecks on a storm-tossed ship; violent motion is a galloping horse, a very rough wagon ride, a small boat in rapids or a storm, or being on deck on a storm-tossed ship; extraordinarily violent motion is anything worse, such as an earthquake. Bad weather is high wind carrying blinding rain or sleet; severe weather is wind-driven hail, dust, or debris. Outcomes: on a success you continue normally; on a failure the act fails and the effort is wasted — a spell being cast is lost, concentrating on an active spell ends it as if you had ceased concentrating, directing a spell fails (but the spell stays active), a spell-like ability use is lost, and a skill use fails, possibly with further ramifications. Casting or acting defensively: you can make a Concentration check (DC 15 + the spell's level when casting) to cast a spell, use a spell-like ability, or use a skill without provoking attacks of opportunity for that act; on a failure the act also fails with the usual consequences. A successful defensive check doesn't excuse any other check the stressful situation requires, and defensive casting cannot be applied to other provoking acts unless the activity's description says so.",
