    id: "rc-conditions",
    name: "Conditions",
    section: "C",
    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "If more than one condition affects a creature, apply them all; if certain effects cannot combine, apply the most severe. The conditions, with their effects: Ability Damaged — ability scores reduced by ability damage (see Ability Score Loss). Ability Drained — ability scores reduced by ability drain (see Ability Score Loss). Blinded — cannot see: -2 AC, denied Dexterity bonus to AC, moves at half speed, -4 on Search checks and on most Strength- and Dexterity-based skill checks; all checks and activities relying on vision (such as reading and Spot checks) automatically fail; all opponents have total concealment from the blinded creature; it is immune to gaze attacks and other attacks that require the target to see. Blown Away — by wind, depending on size: a creature on the ground is knocked down and rolls 1d4x10 feet, taking 1d4 points of nonlethal damage per 10 feet each round; a flying creature is blown back 2d6x10 feet and takes 2d6 points of nonlethal damage per round. Checked — wind prevents forward motion: grounded creatures merely stop; flying creatures are moved back as the effect specifies. Confused — at the start of each of its turns, roll d%: 01-10 attack the caster with melee or ranged weapons (or close with the caster if attacking isn't possible); 11-20 act normally; 21-50 do nothing but babble incoherently; 51-70 flee from the caster at top possible speed; 71-100 attack the nearest creature (a familiar counts as part of the subject itself). A confused creature that cannot carry out the indicated activity does nothing but babble; attackers gain no special advantage against it; it always retaliates against its attackers on its next turn if still confused; and it makes no attacks of opportunity against creatures it isn't already devoted to attacking (by its most recent attack, or by having just been attacked). Cowering — frozen in fear or awe: takes no actions, -2 AC, and loses its Dexterity bonus for all purposes. Dazed — can take no actions but takes no penalty to AC; typically lasts 1 round. Dazzled — -1 on attack rolls, Search checks, and Spot checks. Dead — see Injury, Healing, and Death (p. 72; outside this partial copy). Deafened — cannot hear: -4 on initiative checks, automatically fails Listen checks, and has a 20% spell failure chance on spells with verbal components. Dehydrated — a creature that has taken nonlethal damage from lack of water: it becomes fatigued, and nonlethal damage it would take from hot conditions (p. 155) instead becomes lethal. Disabled — see Injury, Healing, and Death (p. 72; outside this partial copy). Dying — see Injury, Healing, and Death (p. 72; outside this partial copy). Energy Drained — suffering one or more negative levels (see Energy Drain and Level Loss). Entangled — ensnared: moves at half speed (unless the entangling effect anchors or tethers it, which can prevent movement entirely), cannot run or charge, -2 on attack rolls, -4 Dexterity; casting a spell while entangled requires a Concentration check (DC 15 + spell level). Exhausted — cannot run or charge, -6 to Strength and Dexterity, moves at half speed; after 1 hour of complete rest, an exhausted creature becomes fatigued. Fascinated — entranced: stands or sits quietly, taking no actions other than paying attention to the fascinating effect; -4 on skill checks made as reactions (such as Listen and Spot); a potential threat (such as a hostile creature approaching) allows a new saving throw; an obvious threat (a weapon drawn, a spell cast, a ranged weapon aimed at it) automatically breaks the effect; another creature can free it as a standard action. Fatigued — cannot run or charge, -2 to Strength and Dexterity; anything that would normally cause fatigue makes a fatigued creature exhausted instead; 8 hours of complete rest removes the condition. Flat-Footed — hasn't yet acted in the combat: denied Dexterity bonus to AC and cannot make attacks of opportunity. Frightened — see Fear (p. 53; outside this partial copy). Grappling — its actions are limited (see Grappling, p. 60; outside this partial copy); it threatens no squares and is denied its Dexterity bonus to AC against opponents it isn't grappling. Helpless — paralyzed, held, bound, sleeping, unconscious, or otherwise completely at an opponent's mercy: treated as having Dexterity 0 (-5 modifier), -4 AC against melee attacks, vulnerable to attacks that rely on the target being denied its Dexterity bonus, and an enemy can deliver a coup de grace against it with a melee weapon as a full-round action. Immobilized — cannot move out of the space it occupied when immobilized, but otherwise functions normally unless flying: immobilized flyers that can hover hold their altitude, and all others descend 20 feet per round until they reach the ground, taking no falling damage. Incorporeal — see Incorporeality (p. 64; outside this partial copy). Invisible — see Invisibility (p. 76; outside this partial copy). Knocked Down — by wind, depending on size: grounded creatures are knocked prone; flying creatures are blown back 1d6x10 feet. Nauseated — unable to attack, cast spells, concentrate on spells, or do anything else requiring attention; the only action it can take on its turn is a single move action; affects only living creatures (not construct, deathless, or undead types). Panicked — see Fear (p. 53; outside this partial copy). Paralyzed — frozen and helpless: effective Strength and Dexterity scores of 0, though purely mental activities are still possible; a winged creature flying when paralyzed cannot flap its wings and falls; a paralyzed swimmer cannot swim and might drown. Petrified — turned to stone and considered unconscious: if the petrified body cracks or breaks but the pieces are rejoined when it returns to flesh, the creature is unharmed; if the body is incomplete when it returns to flesh, the body is likewise incomplete, and the DM decides on permanent hit point loss and/or debilitation. Pinned — held stationary (but not helpless) for 1 round: can take only the actions the pinning creature allows (even speaking is restricted), -4 AC against opponents other than the pinner, cannot move, so its Dexterity counts as 0 for AC (-5 modifier), and it is vulnerable to attacks that rely on denial of the Dexterity bonus (such as sneak attack). Prone — lying flat (any creature that can flatten itself, even a limbless snake, can choose to be prone even if it cannot reasonably be tripped): a prone attacker takes -4 on melee attack rolls and cannot use ranged weapons except crossbows and shurikens; a prone defender gains +4 AC against ranged attacks and takes -4 AC against melee attacks; standing up is a move action that provokes attacks of opportunity. Rebuked — cowering in awe for 10 rounds as a result of a rebuke undead attempt. Shaken — see Fear (p. 53; outside this partial copy). Sickened — physical discomfort: -2 on attack rolls, damage rolls, saving throws, skill checks, and ability checks; affects only living creatures (not construct, deathless, or undead types). Stable — see Injury, Healing, and Death (p. 72; outside this partial copy). Staggered — when its nonlethal damage exactly equals its current hit points: it can take a single move action or standard action each round (not both, and no full-round actions); nonlethal damage exceeding current hit points causes unconsciousness instead. Stunned — drops everything held, can take no actions, -2 AC, denied Dexterity bonus to AC. Turned — flees for 10 rounds (1 minute) by the best and fastest means available after a turn undead attempt; if it cannot flee, it cowers. Unconscious — knocked out and helpless.",
