    ruleset: "core",
    summary:
      "Counterspelling is the active process of using one spell to negate the casting of another, usually in combat; any spell can be cast as a counterspell, and counterspelling works even if one spell is divine and the other arcane. To use a counterspell, select an opponent as its target by readying a standard action: you wait to complete your action until the opponent tries to cast a spell, and you can still take a move action, since readying is a standard action. If the target tries to cast a spell, make a Spellcraft check (DC 15 + the spell's level); this check doesn't require an action. On a success you correctly identify the spell and can attempt to counter it; on a failure you can do neither. To complete the counter you must then cast the correct spell — a spell can counter only another spell of the same name. If you can cast such a spell and have it prepared (if you prepare spells), you cast it, altering it slightly to create the counterspell effect; if the target is within range, both spells negate each other with no other results. Metamagic feats aren't taken into account when determining whether a spell can be countered. Some spells counter each other, especially ones with diametrically opposed effects; a spell's description says whether it can be used to counter another spell. Dispel magic can be used as a counterspell, and you don't need to identify the spell the other spellcaster is casting — but dispel magic doesn't always work as a counterspell (see that spell's description).",
