    summary:
      "A critical hit is an attack that deals extra damage by striking a vital area; certain creatures are immune to the extra damage because they have no vital organs, points of weakness, or differentiation between parts of the body. Determining one: when an attack roll comes up a natural 20, you hit regardless of the target's Armor Class and have scored a threat. To find out whether it is a critical hit, immediately make a critical roll — another attack roll with all the same modifiers as the attack roll just made. If the critical roll also hits the target's AC, the original hit is a critical hit (the critical roll only needs to hit; it doesn't need to be another 20); if the critical roll misses, the hit is just a regular hit. Increased threat range: some attacks threaten on a natural roll lower than 20 (a longsword threatens on 19 or 20), but a roll lower than 20 is not an automatic hit, and any attack roll that doesn't hit is not a threat. Results: on a critical hit you roll the damage more than once, with all your usual bonuses, and add the rolls together; unless otherwise specified, the threat range is 20 and the multiplier is x2. Extra damage beyond the weapon's normal damage — such as precision damage or the extra damage from a flaming weapon — isn't multiplied on a critical hit. Increased critical multiplier: some weapons, such as battleaxes and arrows, deal more than double damage on a critical hit. Spells and critical hits: a spell that requires an attack roll can score a critical hit; a spell that requires no attack roll cannot, even if it deals damage.",
