    summary:
      "Encumbrance rules determine how much a creature can carry before slowing down; a creature can be encumbered by armor or by weight. Encumbrance by armor: a creature's armor defines its armor check penalty, overall speed, and running speed, and unless the creature is weak or carrying a lot of gear, that is all you need to know — extra gear doesn't slow it further. Encumbrance by weight: total the weight of everything carried and worn and compare it to the Carrying Capacity table for the creature's Strength to find a light, medium, or heavy load; a light load doesn't encumber. For a Medium biped, the maximum light load by Strength 1-29 is: 3, 6, 10, 13, 16, 20, 23, 26, 30, 33, 38, 43, 50, 58, 66, 76, 86, 100, 116, 133, 153, 173, 200, 233, 266, 306, 346, 400, and 466 lb.; the maximum medium load is twice the light maximum and the maximum heavy load is three times it. Carrying loads: a medium load caps your Dexterity bonus to AC at +3, imposes a -3 check penalty (which works like an armor check penalty), reduces speed to 20 feet from a 30-foot base (15 feet from a 20-foot base), and limits running to x3; a heavy load caps the Dexterity bonus at +1, imposes a -6 check penalty, reduces speed the same way, and allows running at x4. Reduced speeds for higher base speeds (medium or heavy load): 40 becomes 30, 50 becomes 35, 60 becomes 40, 70 becomes 50, 80 becomes 55, 90 becomes 60, 100 becomes 70 feet. A medium or heavy load also counts as medium or heavy armor for abilities and skills restricted by armor. If a creature is both wearing armor and carrying a load, use the worst figure in each category — the penalties don't stack. Size and shape: the Carrying Capacity figures are for Medium bipeds; larger bipeds multiply by size — Large x2, Huge x4, Gargantuan x8, Colossal x16 — and smaller bipeds carry less: Small x3/4, Tiny x1/2, Diminutive x1/4, Fine x1/8. Quadrupeds use different multipliers: Fine x1/4, Diminutive x1/2, Tiny x3/4, Small x1, Medium x1-1/2, Large x3, Huge x6, Gargantuan x12, Colossal x24. Tremendous Strength: for Strength scores beyond the table, find the score between 20 and 29 with the same ones digit and multiply its figures by 4 if the creature's Strength is in the 30s, by 16 in the 40s, by 64 in the 50s, and so on. Lifting and dragging: a creature can lift up to its maximum load over its head, and can lift up to double its maximum load off the ground — while doing so it is denied its Dexterity bonus to AC and can move only 5 feet per round as a full-round action. A creature can push or drag along the ground as much as five times its maximum load; favorable conditions (smooth ground, a slick object) can double these numbers, and bad circumstances (broken ground, an object that snags) can reduce them to half or lower.",
